  "answer_text": "Great question! What you're seeing is completely normal and harmless. Here's a breakdown:\n\n## What It Is: Limescale (Mineral Deposits)\n\nWhat you're seeing is a natural and completely harmless phenomenon called **limescale**, or calcium scale. Limescale is a chalky white deposit made primarily of **calcium carbonate**, along with smaller amounts of magnesium salts.\n\n## Why It Happens\n\nThese minerals naturally occur in water, especially in what's commonly known as \"hard water.\" When water evaporates or sits, these minerals are left behind on surfaces \u2014 like the walls of your glass.\n\nEven though you're using a filtered fridge water, water filters, particularly those using activated carbon, are designed to remove impurities such as chlorine, sediment, and certain organic compounds \u2014 but they don't always remove all minerals. So some calcium and magnesium can still make it through.\n\n## Is It Harmful?\n\nThe white film in filtered water is typically calcium carbonate, a harmless mineral deposit. While aesthetically unappealing, it poses no health risks and is a common occurrence.\n\n## How to Clean It\n\nThere are a few easy, natural solutions:\n\n- **White vinegar:** For light buildup, a simple soak with a mixture of white vinegar and warm water will do the trick.\n- **Baking soda or lemon juice:** You can use baking soda or lemon juice along with a lint-free cloth to rub limescale off your glass \u2014 this method may take some elbow grease, but it gives your glassware a thorough clean.\n\n## How to Reduce It\n\nReducing water hardness levels will require investing in a water softener or reverse osmosis treatment system. Otherwise, regular rinsing and cleaning is the easiest way to manage it.\n\nIn short \u2014 it's just the natural minerals in your water concentrating on the glass surface over time. Totally safe!",
